### 3\. Theory and Concepts

### 3.1 Binary Search Tree (BST)

A Binary Search Tree (BST) is a hierarchical, non-linear data structure in which each node contains a data value and references to its left and right child nodes. A BST follows a specific ordering property that makes searching, insertion and deletion operations efficient.

In a Binary Search Tree, the left child contains a value smaller than its parent node, while the right child contains a value greater than its parent node. This property is maintained throughout the tree.

The first node inserted into the tree is called the root node. Every node can have a maximum of two children, which are known as the left child and right child. Nodes without any children are called leaf nodes.

